{"step_index":91,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:47:35Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:47:35+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:47:35+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity/sandbox.sh`\nTotal Lines: 84\nTotal Bytes: 3776\nShowing lines 1 to 84\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: #!/bin/bash\n2: # sandbox.sh — run a command in a bubblewrap sandbox that contains only what an\n3: # Antigravity attempt needs.\n4: #\n5: #   sandbox.sh --ws DIR --home DIR --part PARTITION [--ro PATH]... -- CMD [ARGS...]\n6: #\n7: # Why this exists: agy's permission rules confine its shell commands but not its\n8: # file tools. The permission probe (job 84813) had agents write, edit and read\n9: # outside the workspace under every rule form, with no permission check logged.\n10: # So the boundary has to be the filesystem the agents can see, and this script\n11: # builds it:\n12: #\n13: #   writable   the workspace (--ws) and the private agy home (--home)\n14: #   read-only  /usr /etc and the libc trees; agy; the Lean toolchain and\n15: #              lean_project (a stray `lake` cannot delete Mathlib); the checker,\n16: #              its Python venv and code; this partition's challenges\n17: #   absent     everything else — the rest of $HOME (~/.ssh, the real OAuth token,\n18: #              other projects), the repository's results and handoff trees, and\n19: #              /run, which is where the munge socket lives: without it nothing in\n20: #              here can submit SLURM jobs\n21: #\n22: # agy's own policy file inside --home stays writable: agy rewrites it atomically\n23: # at startup, and a read-only mount on top broke that (probe 84823). It is only a\n24: # second layer — an agent that widened agy's rules would still be confined to\n25: # this filesystem. The real agy config directory is not mounted at all: the\n26: # private home carries its own copies of what agy needs. Network is shared: agy\n27: # needs it.\n28: set -euo pipefail\n29: ROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$(readlink -f \"$0\")\")/../..\" && pwd)\"\n30: ENVF=\"$ROOT/handoff/.agf_env\"\n31: agf() { grep -m1 \"^$1=\" \"$ENVF\" | cut -d= -f2-; }\n32: AGY=\"$(agf AGF_AGY)\"; ELAN=\"$(agf AGF_ELAN_HOME)\"; CFG=\"$(agf AGF_AGY_CONFIG)\"\n33: \n34: WS=\"\"; HOMEDIR=\"\"; PART=\"\"; EXTRA=()\n35: while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do\n36:   case \"$1\" in\n37:     --ws)   WS=\"$(readlink -f \"$2\")\"; shift 2 ;;\n38:     --home) HOMEDIR=\"$(readlink -f \"$2\")\"; shift 2 ;;\n39:     --part) PART=\"$2\"; shift 2 ;;\n40:     --ro)   p=\"$(readlink -f \"$2\")\"; EXTRA+=(--ro-bind \"$p\" \"$p\"); shift 2 ;;\n41:     --)     shift; break ;;\n42:     *) echo \"sandbox.sh: unknown argument $1\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n43:   esac\n44: done\n45: if [ -z \"$WS\" ] || [ -z \"$HOMEDIR\" ] || [ -z \"$PART\" ] || [ $# -eq 0 ]; then\n46:   echo \"usage: sandbox.sh --ws DIR --home DIR --part PARTITION [--ro PATH]... -- CMD\" >&2; exit 2\n47: fi\n48: [ -d \"$WS\" ] && [ -d \"$HOMEDIR\" ] || { echo \"sandbox.sh: --ws and --home must exist\" >&2; exit 2; }\n49: case \"$WS/\" in\n50:   \"$ROOT/lean_project/\"*|\"$ROOT/tools/\"*|\"$ROOT/.venv/\"*|\"$ELAN/\"*|\"$CFG/\"*)\n51:     echo \"sandbox.sh: refusing a workspace inside a protected tree\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\n52: esac\n53: \n54: SYS=(--ro-bind /usr /usr --ro-bind /etc /etc)\n55: for d in /bin /sbin /lib /lib64; do\n56:   if [ -L \"$d\" ]; then SYS+=(--symlink \"$(readlink \"$d\")\" \"$d\")\n57:   elif [ -d \"$d\" ]; then SYS+=(--ro-bind \"$d\" \"$d\"); fi\n58: done\n59: \n60: exec bwrap \\\n61:   --die-with-parent --new-session \\\n62:   --unshare-pid --unshare-ipc --unshare-uts --unshare-cgroup-try \\\n63:   \"${SYS[@]}\" \\\n64:   --proc /proc --dev /dev \\\n65:   --tmpfs /tmp --tmpfs /var/tmp --tmpfs /run \\\n66:   --ro-bind \"$AGY\" \"$AGY\" \\\n67:   --ro-bind \"$ELAN\" \"$ELAN\" \\\n68:   --ro-bind \"$ROOT/lean_project\" \"$ROOT/lean_project\" \\\n69:   --ro-bind \"$ROOT/.venv\" \"$ROOT/.venv\" \\\n70:   --ro-bind \"$ROOT/tools\" \"$ROOT/tools\" \\\n71:   --ro-bind \"$ROOT/pipeline\" \"$ROOT/pipeline\" \\\n72:   --ro-bind \"$ROOT/config.py\" \"$ROOT/config.py\" \\\n73:   --ro-bind \"$ROOT/benchmark/challenges_$PART\" \"$ROOT/benchmark/challenges_$PART\" \\\n74:   --ro-bind \"$ENVF\" \"$ENVF\" \\\n75:   --ro-bind \"$ROOT/handoff/.agf_lean_path\" \"$ROOT/handoff/.agf_lean_path\" \\\n76:   \"${EXTRA[@]}\" \\\n77:   --bind \"$HOMEDIR\" \"$HOMEDIR\" \\\n78:   --bind \"$WS\" \"$WS\" \\\n79:   --chdir \"$WS\" \\\n80:   --clearenv \\\n81:   --setenv HOME \"$HOMEDIR\" --setenv PATH /usr/bin:/bin \\\n82:   --setenv USER \"$(id -un)\" --setenv LANG C.UTF-8 --setenv TMPDIR /tmp \\\n83:   -- \"$@\"\n84: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":92,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:47:35Z","thinking":"**Examining sandbox bindings and script**\n\nThe sandbox utilizes specific bindings, including the workspace directory and home directory, along with several read-only binds for project components. Investigating the `tools/antigravity_handoff.py` script further, the `check.sh` script executes this file with linting functionality, and then with a Lean command.\n\n\n**Deconstructing the Candidate's proof**\n\nExamining if `prove_2a2555cc` is provable requires analyzing the structure of `Candidate`. Breaking down `Candidate` reveals its core: proving the radius is less than or equal to the total domination number for all graphs meeting certain conditions. The definitions of radius and total domination number need examination within this context.\n\n\n**Analyzing `Finset.min` behavior**\n\nThe definition of `totalDominationNumber` uses `minCard`, which in turn relies on `Finset.min`. Considering that `s.min` returns an `Option ℕ` and is used with `.getD 0`, the default value will be 0 if the set is empty. The properties of `Finset.min` in Mathlib need to be fully understood, especially when the set is empty or contains multiple elements. Examining its interaction with `Option.getD` is crucial. The properties `Finset.min_le` are relevant.\n\nI'm thinking through how to approach this.\n\n\n**Formulating a provable strategy**\n\nAttempting to prove that the graph's radius is less than or equal to the total domination number. The check script verifies against an original, with no modifications to the theorem header allowed. Focusing on proving a related statement: if *S* is any total dominating set, then graph radius is less than or equal to the cardinality of *S*. If *S* is any total dominating set, the radius is less than or equal to the size of *S*. The definition of total domination number is therefore the minimum size of a total dominating set, and this implies that the radius is less than or equal to the total domination number.\n\n\n**Directly proving the radius bound**\n\nConsidering how to show `radius G ≤ s.card` directly from `IsTotalDominatingSet G s`. Need to demonstrate that for any total dominating set $S$, there's a vertex $v$ with `ballWithin G s.card v = univ`. This implies that for any total dominating set *S* of *G*, there exists a vertex *v* such that the eccentricity of *v* is less than or equal to the cardinality of *S*.\n\nLet's test this conjecture with examples. For $P_8$ with total dominating set {2,3,6,7}, $|S|=4$. Finding vertices 4 or 5 with eccentricity ≤ 4. Similarly, for larger or complete sets in $P_8$ and $P_{12}$, the condition holds. Testing further with $P_{10}$ to verify this hypothesis.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/theorem_prover.py","EndLine":100,"StartLine":1,"toolAction":"Viewing file","toolSummary":"View theorem_prover.py"}}]}
{"step_index":93,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:47:46Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:47:46+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:47:48+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/theorem_prover.py`\nTotal Lines: 890\nTotal Bytes: 40615\nShowing lines 1 to 100\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: \"\"\"\n2: pipeline/theorem_prover.py — Stage 4: Automated Theorem Proving\n3: \n4: Provides a clean interface to neural theorem provers (Goedel-Prover,\n5: DeepSeek-Prover-V2).  These systems are cutting-edge research prototypes\n6: with no stable public API as of 2025; the implementations below are\n7: well-documented stubs that wire the correct request/response schema.\n8: \n9: To connect a real prover:\n10:   1. Set cfg.prover_api_url and cfg.prover_api_key in config.py (or env vars).\n11:   2. Replace the `_STUB_RESPONSE` logic in `_call_prover_api` with real HTTP calls.\n12:   3. The response schema is documented in `ProverResponse`.\n13: \n14: The module also supports a `LeanSubprocessProver` that tries to search for\n15: a proof using the `lean` binary + `exact?` / `decide` tactics (works for\n16: very simple arithmetic statements).\n17: \"\"\"\n18: \n19: from __future__ import annotations\n20: \n21: import json\n22: import logging\n23: import os\n24: import shutil\n25: import subprocess\n26: import tempfile\n27: import textwrap\n28: import time\n29: from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n30: from pathlib import Path\n31: from typing import List, Optional\n32: \n33: from config import Config, CONFIG\n34: from conjecture import Conjecture, ConjectureStatus\n35: from pipeline import proof_audit\n36: \n37: logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n38: \n39: \n40: # Markers in Lean's output that signal an incomplete / uncertified proof. A zero\n41: # exit code alone is NOT sufficient: inside a lake project `apply?`/`exact?` leave\n42: # \"Try this\" suggestions with unsolved subgoals yet `lean` may still exit 0, and\n43: # `sorry`/`admit` only warn. (This is the false-positive that once mis-certified a\n44: # false `domination = slater` theorem.)\n45: _BAD_PROOF_MARKERS = (\"sorry\", \"Try this\", \"unsolved goals\", \"found a partial proof\",\n46:                       \"error:\", \"declaration uses\")\n47: \n48: \n49: def _proof_certified(returncode: int, lean_output: str) -> bool:\n50:     \"\"\"True only for a genuinely kernel-checked proof: clean (zero) exit AND no\n51:     marker of an incomplete/uncertified proof in Lean's output.\"\"\"\n52:     return returncode == 0 and not any(m in lean_output for m in _BAD_PROOF_MARKERS)\n53: \n54: \n55: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n56: # Prover response schema\n57: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n58: \n59: @dataclass\n60: class ProverResponse:\n61:     \"\"\"\n62:     Expected response from a neural theorem prover API.\n63: \n64:     Fields match what Goedel-Prover-SB / DeepSeek-Prover-V2 would return\n65:     (schema inferred from published papers; adapt when APIs go public).\n66:     \"\"\"\n67: \n68:     success: bool = False\n69:     proof_text: Optional[str] = None       # complete Lean 4 proof term\n70:     proof_tactics: Optional[str] = None    # tactic proof (if available)\n71:     model_name: str = \"\"\n72:     tokens_used: int = 0\n73:     elapsed_s: float = 0.0\n74:     error: Optional[str] = None\n75: \n76:     # Internal routing\n77:     _stub: bool = False\n78: \n79:     def to_dict(self) -> dict:\n80:         return {\n81:             \"success\": self.success,\n82:             \"proof_text\": self.proof_text,\n83:             \"proof_tactics\": self.proof_tactics,\n84:             \"model_name\": self.model_name,\n85:             \"tokens_used\": self.tokens_used,\n86:             \"elapsed_s\": self.elapsed_s,\n87:             \"error\": self.error,\n88:             \"stub\": self._stub,\n89:         }\n90: \n91: \n92: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n93: # Base class\n94: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n95: \n96: class BaseProver:\n97:     \"\"\"Abstract interface; concrete provers inherit and override `prove_one`.\"\"\"\n98: \n99:     name: str = \"base\"\n100: \nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
